<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Sotuta</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1546 CE (approx.) · capital Sotuta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Maya province of the Cocom lineage in central Yucatán, bitter enemies of the Xiu and leaders of resistance to the Spanish conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a> had been the leading lineage of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a>-led revolt destroyed Mayapan around 1441 and massacred the Cocom, a surviving son established a new realm centred on Sotuta in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sotuta was ruled by a halach uinic (supreme lord) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a> lineage over the batabs of its towns. Its feud with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mani">Maní</a> dominated regional politics: in 1536 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nachi-cocom">Nachi Cocom</a> killed a Xiu embassy, including the lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ah-dzun-xiu">Ah Dzun Xiu</a>, at Otzmal while they travelled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>. Sotuta refused alliance with the Spanish.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sotuta was among the last provinces to submit and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-maya-revolt">Great Maya Revolt</a> of 1546-47, which was crushed. It was then absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nachi-cocom">Nachi Cocom</a> later accepted baptism as Juan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sotuta.jpg" alt="Sotuta" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Jaontiveros · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacicazgos_mayas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1546 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sotuta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nachi Cocom</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cocom province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1494&amp;focus=sotuta" aria-label="Sotuta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2283 1416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sotuta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2603 1672L2591 1670M2603 1672L4910 989M2603 1672L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2603 1672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2603 1672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1494&amp;focus=sotuta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
